|> Exercise
    handle=_complete_square_1_

    Show that
    $$
    x^2 + 10x + 30
    $$
    can be written in the form
    $$
    (x + \dots)^2 + \,\dots
    $$
    for some numbers “$\dots$” and “$\dots$”.

    |> Solution

        The answer is
        $$
        (x + 5)^2 + 5
        $$
        because
        $$
        \begin{align}
        \,\,\,\,(x + 5)^2
        &= x^2 + (2\cdot 5)x + 5^2 \\
        &= \up{1.4} x^2 + 10x + 25
        \end{align}
        $$
        and adding $5$ gives $x^2 + 10x + 30$.
